WEBVTT

00:00.120 --> 00:00.953
Instructor: So next up,

00:00.953 --> 00:02.970
I wanna show you how we can turn to the reflogs

00:02.970 --> 00:05.040
in times of need.

00:05.040 --> 00:07.440
In this example video, I'll show you how we could

00:07.440 --> 00:11.010
reset, do a hard reset and lose some commits,

00:11.010 --> 00:14.760
but we can still retrieve the data from the reflog.

00:14.760 --> 00:18.000
So let's make a simple situation.

00:18.000 --> 00:19.800
I was working in that react repo.

00:19.800 --> 00:22.440
It just had so many files and commits.

00:22.440 --> 00:25.620
It's a little bit more confusing to take a look at.

00:25.620 --> 00:28.200
So I'm gonna do something simple, you can follow along.

00:28.200 --> 00:29.550
I'm not in a repo.

00:29.550 --> 00:31.110
I'm going to init,

00:31.110 --> 00:33.330
and I'm gonna just track the seeds

00:33.330 --> 00:36.270
that I've planted in my garden or something like that.

00:36.270 --> 00:38.400
So why don't I make a file

00:38.400 --> 00:42.450
I'll call this one veggies.txt, okay?

00:42.450 --> 00:46.080
And I'll add that file, git add veggies,

00:46.080 --> 00:51.080
git commits -m add veggies file.

00:51.450 --> 00:53.880
I'm just gonna build up two or three commits.

00:53.880 --> 00:55.410
So I open this in my editor

00:55.410 --> 00:58.740
I'll do code dot from my terminal, that's just the shortcut.

00:58.740 --> 01:01.950
And I don't have any files except for veggies.

01:01.950 --> 01:03.840
So what should I put in here?

01:03.840 --> 01:06.840
Well, I have been planting some things recently

01:06.840 --> 01:09.150
so I'll just put some of the stuff I started.

01:09.150 --> 01:13.060
We have broccoli and rainbow chard

01:14.070 --> 01:16.950
and what else did I plant recently?

01:16.950 --> 01:18.750
Some kale, boring.

01:18.750 --> 01:23.460
All right, so now I'll add my first commit, git commit -m.

01:23.460 --> 01:26.730
I'll do am so I don't have to add that file again.

01:26.730 --> 01:29.550
And I'll do a commit message

01:29.550 --> 01:33.603
like plant winter veggies.

01:35.160 --> 01:38.430
Okay, and then I don't know some other stuff.

01:38.430 --> 01:43.323
How about cabbage and some lettuce maybe, arugula.

01:44.580 --> 01:46.770
Did I spell that right, Arugula?

01:46.770 --> 01:48.750
No, I think it's like that.

01:48.750 --> 01:53.254
So then I'll commit, git commit -am, add more greens.

01:53.254 --> 01:56.040
Okay, and then we're going to do a last commit.

01:56.040 --> 01:57.360
Let's say I get ahead of myself

01:57.360 --> 01:59.250
and I'm just daydreaming about

01:59.250 --> 02:01.560
when I can actually start watermelon,

02:01.560 --> 02:03.450
it's the middle of winter right now,

02:03.450 --> 02:05.460
so not a good time for that.

02:05.460 --> 02:10.410
And pumpkins, and what's another summery thing

02:10.410 --> 02:11.243
that I grow?

02:11.243 --> 02:12.273
Well, squash.

02:13.110 --> 02:14.820
Okay, so if these are things

02:14.820 --> 02:17.520
that I'm looking forward to growing, I get ahead of myself.

02:17.520 --> 02:18.510
I add and commit them.

02:18.510 --> 02:20.880
So git commit -am,

02:20.880 --> 02:24.630
add summer veggies/fruits.

02:24.630 --> 02:28.380
I guess it's not just veggies, summer seeds.

02:28.380 --> 02:32.190
-: Okay, then I decide, oh you idiots,

02:32.190 --> 02:34.170
you can't plant those right now, it's the dead of winter,

02:34.170 --> 02:35.760
get that off of your list.

02:35.760 --> 02:38.460
So this is all very silly obviously,

02:38.460 --> 02:42.120
but imagine I've done a lot of work on some project.

02:42.120 --> 02:43.860
I wanna get rid of that commit.

02:43.860 --> 02:45.213
We've seen how to do this.

02:46.110 --> 02:46.980
You may have forgotten,

02:46.980 --> 02:50.310
because it is something that doesn't come up all that often.

02:50.310 --> 02:52.890
But to completely undo a commit,

02:52.890 --> 02:55.500
we pick some point that we wanna go back to.

02:55.500 --> 02:58.140
So let's do git log oneline,

02:58.140 --> 03:00.390
and let's go back to this point.

03:00.390 --> 03:02.100
So we'll take that commit hash,

03:02.100 --> 03:05.250
and then we can do a git reset, hard.

03:05.250 --> 03:07.830
So --hard with that commit hash

03:07.830 --> 03:09.810
and the hard will undo the changes

03:09.810 --> 03:11.580
in my working directory as well.

03:11.580 --> 03:13.590
So we get rid of the commit,

03:13.590 --> 03:16.110
and then also in this case just one commit

03:16.110 --> 03:18.570
we're resetting back to the prior commit.

03:18.570 --> 03:21.180
But we also lose the work that we had

03:21.180 --> 03:22.860
in our working directory.

03:22.860 --> 03:24.270
And so, it's gone there, right?

03:24.270 --> 03:27.510
If I type git log, that commit is gone.

03:27.510 --> 03:30.480
So if I realize, oh you could still start them,

03:30.480 --> 03:32.070
you have a greenhouse, I wish I did,

03:32.070 --> 03:32.940
I don't have a greenhouse,

03:32.940 --> 03:36.903
but I turn one of my bedrooms into a greenhouse.

03:37.800 --> 03:41.160
If I wanted those changes back, I want that commit,

03:41.160 --> 03:42.600
it's not here, right?

03:42.600 --> 03:44.610
It's not on another branch or anything like that.

03:44.610 --> 03:48.660
It's just not here, git log oneline, nope,

03:48.660 --> 03:49.809
I don't have that.

03:49.809 --> 03:54.809
But thankfully, that information is actually still available

03:54.870 --> 03:56.190
in the reflog.

03:56.190 --> 03:58.860
So if I do git reflog,

03:58.860 --> 04:02.370
I'll just do show master or I could do head if I wanted,

04:02.370 --> 04:04.560
but master will work for us, right?

04:04.560 --> 04:06.660
Because I'm on the master branch by default.

04:06.660 --> 04:08.940
Here is where we were, right?

04:08.940 --> 04:10.800
This is where that commit was.

04:10.800 --> 04:14.550
There's that commit hash FB5072A.

04:14.550 --> 04:16.740
So we have access to that hash.

04:16.740 --> 04:18.090
And here's the information.

04:18.090 --> 04:21.360
This is when I made that commit add summer seeds.

04:21.360 --> 04:24.840
This was one tip ago of the master branch.

04:24.840 --> 04:28.680
So master at one is one way I could access that.

04:28.680 --> 04:31.110
I also have access to the commit hash.

04:31.110 --> 04:33.570
I could check it out for example.

04:33.570 --> 04:34.800
So if I get out of here

04:34.800 --> 04:38.400
I could do a git checkout and paste that hash in.

04:38.400 --> 04:41.040
But remember, I didn't see this in git log.

04:41.040 --> 04:42.840
That does put me in detached head of course.

04:42.840 --> 04:45.180
But here it is, I see it.

04:45.180 --> 04:46.920
So that puts me in detached head.

04:46.920 --> 04:48.320
I'll just switch outta there

04:50.850 --> 04:52.400
and I'll go back to the reflog.

04:53.625 --> 04:55.500
It's still there, right?

04:55.500 --> 04:58.083
It's just not part of my main git log.

04:58.920 --> 05:01.740
This is the history stemming from the commit.

05:01.740 --> 05:03.330
This commit right here, going back

05:03.330 --> 05:05.130
to its parent and to its parent.

05:05.130 --> 05:07.320
The summer seeds commit is just long gone.

05:07.320 --> 05:09.450
But git still stores that information

05:09.450 --> 05:11.460
in the .get folder it,

05:11.460 --> 05:13.890
it will go through and every so often

05:13.890 --> 05:15.780
remove commits that are dangling

05:15.780 --> 05:17.490
that aren't actually connected.

05:17.490 --> 05:20.640
So that the summer seeds commit is not attached

05:20.640 --> 05:21.473
to anything, right?

05:21.473 --> 05:23.910
It doesn't have a parent, it doesn't have child commits.

05:23.910 --> 05:27.720
It's just on its own in terms of the objects directory.

05:27.720 --> 05:29.370
It's just an entry in there.

05:29.370 --> 05:32.730
But because the reflog still references it,

05:32.730 --> 05:34.530
we can go and restore back to it.

05:34.530 --> 05:37.770
So we just checked it out and went into detached head.

05:37.770 --> 05:41.130
But if I want to go back, I wanna make this the actual

05:41.130 --> 05:43.410
new head of my master branch.

05:43.410 --> 05:45.780
In other words, I want to undo the fact

05:45.780 --> 05:48.600
that I removed that commit that I reset.

05:48.600 --> 05:52.380
I can use the commit hash or I can just do master at 1.

05:52.380 --> 05:55.290
If I prefer, I can reference it this way.

05:55.290 --> 05:58.500
These will be referring to the same thing for now, okay?

05:58.500 --> 06:03.500
I can do another git reset hard master at 1,

06:04.380 --> 06:06.060
or I could pass in the commit hash

06:06.060 --> 06:08.449
that we found in the reflog.

06:08.449 --> 06:11.100
And that now says the new tip of this master branch

06:11.100 --> 06:15.390
is whatever master at 1 is from the reflogs.

06:15.390 --> 06:16.983
And if we do a git log here,

06:18.540 --> 06:20.348
we see that we have that commit message back

06:20.348 --> 06:22.830
and we have that hash back.

06:22.830 --> 06:24.202
So it was gone, right?

06:24.202 --> 06:28.380
We didn't see it at all in the git log.

06:28.380 --> 06:31.110
but because we had the ref log available,

06:31.110 --> 06:33.540
we could go back and find that commit hash

06:33.540 --> 06:36.870
or just find the correct line master at 1

06:36.870 --> 06:38.910
and we could reset based upon that.

06:38.910 --> 06:40.950
So even though it looks like we lost the commit

06:40.950 --> 06:43.110
it wasn't tied to any other commits

06:43.110 --> 06:46.410
it was floating on its own, we were able to rescue it.

06:46.410 --> 06:48.150
And that can be extremely useful.

06:48.150 --> 06:50.520
We just undid a hard reset.

06:50.520 --> 06:53.970
But remember, this only works with local changes.

06:53.970 --> 06:56.130
Reflogs only exist for stuff you've done

06:56.130 --> 06:58.440
on your machine and they do expire.

06:58.440 --> 07:00.600
In the next final video on reflogs

07:00.600 --> 07:03.030
I'll show you how we could also undo a rebase

07:03.030 --> 07:05.490
or retrieve commits that we have

07:05.490 --> 07:08.493
to completely rewritten using git rebase.
